COPYRIGHT FIREWALL — ZERO-COPY REWRITE STANDARD (MANDATORY)
================================================================================

The source transcripts are COPYRIGHTED EXPRESSION. Facts inside them are NOT.
Your output must carry the FACTS and carry NONE of the EXPRESSION.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THE FACT / EXPRESSION SPLIT
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

FREE TO USE (facts — not copyrightable):
  ✓ Dates, years, timelines
  ✓ Names of people, places, organisations, laws, operations
  ✓ Numbers, amounts, statistics, casualty counts, distances
  ✓ Sequence of real-world events
  ✓ Publicly documented quotes from officials/courts (attributed, ≤15 words)
  ✓ Names of documents, acts, sections, committees, reports

NEVER USE (expression — copyrightable):
  ✗ Any sentence, clause, or distinctive phrase from a transcript
  ✗ The source's ORDER of explanation, if that order is their creative choice
  ✗ The source's SELECTION of details — which niche specifics it chose to
    include, and which it left out (protected even when fully reworded)
  ✗ The source's PROPORTIONS — how much weight it gave each detail
  ✗ The source's analogies, metaphors, jokes, or comparisons
  ✗ The source's rhetorical questions, exact wording
  ✗ The source's segment structure or chapter framing
  ✗ The source's narrative devices (its signature hooks, motifs, the way it
    introduces a person or plants a name for later)
  ✗ The source's speculation and atmosphere — anything it imagined beyond
    the documented facts
  ✗ Sponsor/ad content (delete entirely)
  ✗ Channel catchphrases, sign-offs, or personal asides
  ✗ The source's emphasis choices where they are editorial opinion

The rule of thumb: a lawyer comparing your script to the strongest source
should not be able to lay them side by side and follow the same detail
sequence down the page.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THE MANDATORY REWRITE RULES (1–9, with 3B–3E)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

RULE 1 — 7-WORD RULE + DISTINCTIVE-PHRASE BAN (hard limit)
  No 7 consecutive words may match ANY source transcript.
  Exception: unavoidable proper nouns and official names
  (e.g. "Communist Party of India Marxist Leninist", "Forest Conservation Act 1980").
  Also banned: any shorter phrase that is distinctive to one source's narration —
  its signature hooks, invented labels, punchlines, or memorable framings
  (e.g. a source's "remember this name" device, its coined metaphor for a system,
  its dramatised "digital copy of his soul" style image). Facts may stay;
  the source's phrasing of those facts must never travel with them.
  Before finalising any sentence, mentally scan: could this exact string — or
  this signature turn of phrase — exist in a source? If yes, rebuild it from
  a different angle.

RULE 2 — FACTS-TO-SKELETON FIRST
  Do NOT write while reading transcripts. First extract a bare FACT LEDGER:
  date → actor → action → consequence. Then close the transcripts mentally
  and write the script FROM THE LEDGER ONLY. This is the single most
  effective anti-copy mechanism.

RULE 3 — RESEQUENCE DELIBERATELY
  Your narrative order must differ structurally from every source.
  Merging 3–4 sources does NOT create a new structure by itself. Merging only
  resequences facts that appear in more than one source. Wherever a fact exists
  in just one source, there is nothing to merge it against — so if you write it
  down as you read it, you reproduce that source's structure exactly.
  Therefore your root-cause entry point, your midpoint reveal, and your closing
  frame must each be a choice you can defend as your own, and you must run
  RULE 3B on every section the merge cannot fix.

RULE 3B — SINGLE-SOURCE DOMINANCE OVERRIDE (mandatory)
  A story beat is SOURCE-DOMINATED when 80%+ of its specific details (numbers,
  code names, technical mechanisms, minor proper nouns) come from one source.
  In the research file, any source holding a large share of the unique details
  is flagged in the SOURCE DOMINANCE MAP. Read it before writing.

  For EVERY source-dominated beat, all four are mandatory:

  (a) REORDER — Present its details in a different order from the source.
      Do not paraphrase its order; invert it. Lead with the consequence, the
      system, or the human cost, and reach the mechanism afterwards.
      Ask: "if the source explains A → B → C, why is B → A the better
      narration for MY causal chain?" Then write B → A.

  (b) THIN — Cut at least one third of its niche details. Keep only details
      the causal chain genuinely needs. A detail that is merely surprising,
      and carries no causal weight, is the source's editorial selection —
      not yours. Drop it.

  (c) INTERLEAVE — Never let a source-dominated beat run as an unbroken
      stretch of that one source's material. Break it with the human anchor,
      a CONFIRMED fact from another source, or your own mechanism explanation,
      so the beat reads as your synthesis and not as a retelling.

  (d) RE-ENTER — Enter the beat from a different doorway than the source did.
      If the source enters through the technology, you enter through the person
      it was used on, or through the institution that authorised it.

RULE 3C — SELECTION AND SEQUENCE ARE PROTECTED, NOT JUST WORDS
  Copyright protects the selection, sequence and organisation of material,
  not only its wording. A script that names the same unusual details, in the
  same order, in the same proportion as one source is still copying — even
  with 100% original sentences and zero 7-word matches.
  Forbidden, regardless of rewording:
    ✗ Reproducing a source's chain of niche details in its order
    ✗ Reusing a source's signature narrative device (its way of introducing a
      person, its running motif, its "remember this name" style hook)
    ✗ Matching a source's emphasis pattern — the same detail given the same
      dramatic weight at the same point in the story
    ✗ Carrying over a source's cluster of technical specifics as a set
      (if a source pairs four items, use at most two, and place them apart)
  Concrete test — DETAIL-CLUSTER BAN:
    If one source uniquely packages a run such as
    (A firmware bug) → (named satellite stack) → (named AI system + %) →
    (trivial password) → (compound security radii),
    you may keep at most TWO of those niche items, and they must NOT appear
    adjacent or in that source's order. The rest: drop, or replace with a
    CONFIRMED fact from another source, or express as a bare mechanism
    without the source's packaging.
  Required instead:
    ✓ Your own selection: which details earn their place is decided by YOUR
      causal chain, not by which details a source found exciting
    ✓ Your own proportions: what one source treats as its centrepiece may be
      one line in your script, and vice versa
    ✓ Your own devices: every hook, motif and viewer-address is invented here

RULE 3D — NO INHERITED DRAMATISATION
  A source's speculative, atmospheric or embellished framing is its creative
  expression — the most protected material in the transcript, and the least
  reliable. Never carry it over, not even reworded.
  If a source dramatises beyond the documented facts (imagining what a system
  "noticed", what a person "felt", what a room "looked like"), that framing is
  unusable. Return to the bare fact in your ledger and build your own
  dramatisation from it, or state the fact plainly.
  Test: "Could I have written this image if I only had the fact ledger?"
  If no, it came from the source. Delete and rebuild.

RULE 3E — NO FABRICATED FACT-LIKE DETAIL ABOUT REAL PEOPLE
  Dramatisation must not invent verifiable-sounding specifics about real,
  named people — no invented family members, no invented names for relatives,
  no invented private conversations presented as record.
  Allowed: documented people rendered with sensory texture drawn from
  documented circumstances; clearly-signalled reconstruction
  ("उस रात कमरे में क्या हुआ, इसका कोई रिकॉर्ड नहीं है — लेकिन जो दर्ज है, वो ये है…").
  Not allowed: a named daughter, gardener, or child who does not exist in the
  research, narrated as fact. Use the documented human anchor instead, or keep
  the ordinary person unnamed and generic.
  This is an accuracy and defamation guard, separate from copyright.

RULE 4 — ORIGINAL ANALOGIES ONLY
  If a source explains a concept with a comparison, you must invent a
  DIFFERENT comparison — or explain it with plain mechanism instead.
  Never reuse a source's illustrative example.

RULE 5 — NO TRANSCRIPT ARTIFACTS
  Delete: sponsor reads, "link in description", "[संगीत]", "like और subscribe"
  mid-video, auto-caption garble, host banter, guest interview framing,
  channel-specific segment names.

RULE 6 — MULTI-SOURCE CORROBORATION PREFERENCE
  Prefer facts that appear in 2+ transcripts. A fact appearing in only one
  source is either (a) that channel's original research — handle carefully
  and attribute generically ("रिपोर्ट्स के मुताबिक"), or (b) an error.

RULE 7 — CONTRADICTION HANDLING
  When sources disagree on a fact:
    • Prefer the version supported by more sources
    • If still split, present it honestly as disputed:
      "इस पर दो दावे हैं — एक तरफ..., दूसरी तरफ..."
    • Never silently pick a dramatic version over a documented one
  Log every contradiction in META INFO.

RULE 8 — OWN VOICE, OWN SENTENCES
  Write in YOUR calibrated narrator voice (defined below). The Nitish Rajput
  FLOW is a structural method — you replicate the ARCHITECTURE (root-cause,
  causal chain, viewer address, date anchors), not his sentences.
  You must generate fresh phrasings for every connector, hook, and transition.

RULE 9 — QUOTE DISCIPLINE
  Direct quotes allowed ONLY when:
    • Spoken by a public official/court/document on public record
    • Maximum 15 words
    • Clearly attributed ("कोर्ट ने कहा", "गृह मंत्री का बयान था")
    • Maximum 3 such quotes per script
  Never quote a YouTube host. Never quote a transcript's narration.

