================================================================================
RETENTION ENGINE — 6 REASONS VIEWERS STAY (EXPLAINER VERSION)
================================================================================

A viewer decides to stay or leave every 30 seconds.

The six retention types for explainer-documentary:

  1. UNANSWERED QUESTION   — they need to know what happens next
  2. REVEALED INFORMATION  — they just learned something surprising
  3. COMPREHENSION REWARD  — something confusing just became clear
                             (THIS IS THE NITISH RAJPUT SUPERPOWER)
  4. CONSEQUENCE STACK     — pressure is building, stakes are compounding
  5. HUMAN COST            — they feel something for a real person
  6. FEAR OF MISSING OUT   — "what happened next changed everything"

Every 300–350 characters (≈ 30 seconds), at least ONE must activate.

EXPLAINER-SPECIFIC RETENTION BOOSTERS:
  • THE HIDDEN ROOT — "यह असल में शुरू हुआ था [unexpected place/time] से"
  • THE MECHANISM REVEAL — showing exactly HOW a system was exploited
  • THE PAPER-VS-GROUND GAP — the law said one thing, reality was another
  • THE NUMBER PUNCH — a single figure that reframes the scale
  • THE NAMED DOCUMENT — a specific act/report/document as a physical object
  • THE FORWARD PROMISE — a name planted now, detonated later
  • THE PARALLEL TRACK — two forces moving toward collision
  • THE UNINTENDED CONSEQUENCE — a fix that made things worse

================================================================================
30-SECOND RETENTION GRID RULES
================================================================================

  • No same retention TYPE in 3 consecutive blocks.
  • The first 5 blocks (first 2.5 minutes) must each be a DIFFERENT type.
  • Every 6 blocks (3 minutes), insert a COMPREHENSION RESET:
    a short recap bridge ("तो अब तक जो हुआ वो ये था...") that re-anchors the
    viewer before the next escalation.
  • The climax window (final 20%) must run:
    CONSEQUENCE STACK → REVEALED INFO → HUMAN COST → PRESENT-DAY → OPEN QUESTION

================================================================================
THE 7 DROP-OFF DANGER ZONES
================================================================================

DANGER ZONE 1 — THE FIRST 15 SECONDS
  Problem: Opening is slow, generic, or textbook.
  Fix: Open with the MONTAGE — 4–7 standalone shock-lines pulled from later
       in the chain, zero context, ending in a grounding pivot line.
  Test: If line 1 could open a Wikipedia article, rewrite it.

DANGER ZONE 2 — THE ROOT-CAUSE DRAG
  Problem: The origin section (the thing that makes this style great) becomes
           a boring history lecture and viewers leave at minute 2.
  Fix: The root section must carry (a) a surprise, (b) a direct viewer address,
       and (c) an explicit promise that this connects to the main story soon.
       Maximum 6 consecutive sentences of pure background before you either
       land a surprise or explicitly bridge forward.

DANGER ZONE 3 — THE FLAT MIDDLE (minutes 4–8)
  Problem: Plot established, viewer confidence drops.
  Fix: Minutes 4–8 must contain the story's SECOND BIGGEST reveal —
       usually the MECHANISM REVEAL: exactly how the system was worked,
       or the hidden document/decision nobody saw at the time.

DANGER ZONE 4 — THE REPETITIVE CONNECTOR LOOP
  Problem: The same narrative motor phrase used 3+ times.
  Fix: No connector phrase used more than twice. Use the Transition Bank.

DANGER ZONE 5 — THE ABSTRACT LECTURE
  Problem: Concepts, laws, and ideologies become invisible abstractions.
  Fix: Every concept must be shown through a physical anchor an animator can
       draw: a signed document on a desk, a map with a shaded region, a
       ledger, a courtroom stamp, a village well, a factory gate, a file.
  Test: Can an animator draw this scene? If not, add the anchor.

DANGER ZONE 6 — THE FACT AVALANCHE
  Problem: Dates and numbers pile up without consequence, and nothing lands.
  Fix: Every fact must be immediately followed by its consequence.
       Never more than 3 hard facts in a row without a "so what."

DANGER ZONE 7 — THE COLD LANDING
  Problem: The ending arrives without emotional or intellectual preparation.
  Fix: The final 10% must do 5 things in order:
       (a) Pay off the cold open's unanswered question
       (b) Return to the human anchor — what their story means now
       (c) State the present-day status honestly
       (d) Name the systemic lesson without moralising
       (e) Leave ONE genuine open question for the comments

================================================================================
THE COLD OPEN — MONTAGE STANDARD (DEFAULT) + FALLBACKS
================================================================================

▶ TYPE MONTAGE — HOOK-LINE MONTAGE (default; use unless the topic genuinely
   cannot support it)

   A rapid, NON-CHRONOLOGICAL burst of 4–7 standalone shock-lines pulled from
   LATER in the story, stitched with implied hard cuts, BEFORE any
   chronological storytelling begins.

   Rules:
     • 4–7 lines. Each line 1–2 sentences, self-contained.
     • Each line is a shock unit on its own: a name + a shocking act, OR a
       number + a consequence, OR a chilling rhetorical question.
     • Pull lines from ACROSS the whole chain — deliberately out of order.
     • Explain nothing. Resolve nothing. Name no chapter.
     • Do NOT reveal the outcome — tease consequences, never mechanisms.
     • No "आज की कहानी" preamble inside the montage.
     • EVERY montage line must be paid off later at its correct chronological
       position. This is the loop the viewer's brain tracks.
     • Every line must be YOUR original phrasing — never a source's sentence.
     • HARD: do not open with the same beat as any source's cold open.
       Synonym swap of a source opening (same years + same bunker/lift/security
       stack, same "untouchable man" framing, etc.) is still copying.

   MANDATORY PIVOT LINE (ends the montage, starts real narration):
     ONE grounding sentence that shifts tone from chaotic to calm-authoritative
     and opens the chronology — typically a "देखिए..." style opener leading
     into the ROOT, e.g. a line that tells the viewer we are going back to
     where this actually began. Write it fresh every time.

     CRITICAL: The pivot must lead into the ROOT-CAUSE ORIGIN, not into the
     main event. That is what makes this the Nitish Rajput flow.

▶ FALLBACK TYPES (use only with a stated reason in the validator):

   FALLBACK-A — THE IMPOSSIBLE FACT
     Open with one documented fact so counterintuitive it demands explanation.

   FALLBACK-B — THE PRESENT-DAY DOOR
     Open with today's visible situation, then rewind to find its origin.

   FALLBACK-C — THE HUMAN MOMENT
     Open on the human anchor's ordinary day before everything changed.

   All fallbacks still require the PIVOT LINE into the root-cause origin.

❌ NEVER OPEN WITH: a definition, a textbook date, a channel greeting,
   "कुछ कहानियाँ...", a moral statement, or a summary of the whole video.

================================================================================
NARRATIVE ARC — 20-BEAT UNIVERSAL STRUCTURE
No headings in script output
================================================================================

 1.  COLD OPEN MONTAGE — 4–7 non-chronological shock-lines, no explanation.
 2.  PIVOT LINE — one grounding sentence that opens the chronology.
 3.  THE CENTRAL QUESTION — the ONE thing this video will answer, stated plainly.
 4.  THE ROOT — the earliest structural/ideological/economic condition.
     [MANDATORY. May be decades earlier or in another country.]
 5.  ROOT-TO-STORY BRIDGE — explicitly connect the root to our subject,
     with a forward promise that this will matter.
 6.  THE ENABLING CONDITION — what structure/law/power made this possible.
 7.  HUMAN ANCHOR INTRODUCED — one ordinary person, their normal life,
     what they wanted. [Before any conflict escalates.]
 8.  FIRST FRICTION — the first time the system failed someone.
 9.  THE TRIGGER — the single event that changed the trajectory.
10.  ESCALATION ONE — the immediate consequences and who reacted.
11.  INSTITUTIONAL RESPONSE — how the state/authority/organisation reacted.
12.  THE MECHANISM REVEAL — exactly HOW the system was worked or exploited.
     [Second biggest reveal — must land between minutes 4–8.]
13.  COUNTER-ESCALATION — the response to the response.
14.  RECAP BRIDGE — brief re-anchor before the biggest phase.
15.  THE PEAK — the moment of maximum scale/power/damage, with numbers.
16.  THE PAPER-VS-GROUND GAP — what was promised vs what existed on ground.
17.  THE TURNING POINT — what actually changed the outcome, and why.
18.  DECLINE / RESOLUTION — how it wound down, or why it hasn't.
19.  PRESENT-DAY STATUS — where things stand today, honestly.
20.  HUMAN ANCHOR RETURN + SYSTEMIC LESSON + VERDICT CTA (open question) +
     Like/Subscribe CTA + धन्यवाद / जय हिंद

     NOTE: Every montage line from Beat 1 must be explicitly paid off at its
     correct chronological position across Beats 4–19.

     NOTE: Comment CTAs are woven into this arc per the ENGAGEMENT CTA SYSTEM
     frequency table — typically PREDICTION before Beat 12, OPINION after
     Beat 16, and the mandatory VERDICT inside Beat 20.

     NOTE: Beats may be compressed for shorter durations, but Beats 1, 2, 4,
     7, 12, 15, 19, 20 are NEVER droppable.

================================================================================
STORY DNA — 15 UNIVERSAL NON-NEGOTIABLES
================================================================================

 1. ROOT-CAUSE OPENING — the chronology begins at the structural root.
 2. UNBROKEN CAUSAL CHAIN — every event explained as a consequence.
 3. HUMAN ANCHOR — one ordinary person as the doorway, returning 3+ times.
 4. DATE ANCHORS — a date/year/number every 400–600 characters.
 5. DIRECT VIEWER ADDRESS — minimum 1 per 1,200 characters.
 6. FORWARD PROMISES — minimum 3, each explicitly paid off.
 7. NAMED INSTRUMENTS — acts, operations, documents, committees named exactly.
 8. NUMBER PUNCHES — minimum 2 before/after pairs.
 9. PAPER-VS-GROUND CONTRAST — used at least twice where facts support it.
10. RECAP BRIDGES — 2–4 across the script.
11. PRESENT TENSE DEFAULT — events narrated in present tense.
12. SYSTEMIC DIAGNOSIS — explain structure, don't just assign blame.
13. COMMENT CTAs — per the duration frequency table, never forced, each at a
    genuine story-created ambiguity; VERDICT CTA always present.
14. PRESENT-DAY LANDING — the story reaches today.
15. OPEN QUESTION ENDING — judgment handed to the viewer, not delivered.

================================================================================
THE SENTENCE RHYTHM SYSTEM
================================================================================

SHORT (4–8 words)    = shock, revelation, consequence, turn
MEDIUM (9–18 words)  = causal explanation, building, context
LONG (19–30 words)   = mechanism explanation, atmosphere, emotional weight

Explainer rhythm signature:
  LONG (set up the mechanism) → MEDIUM (what happened) →
  SHORT (the consequence) → SHORT (the punch) → MEDIUM (what it means next)

Climax sequence: SHORT → MEDIUM → SHORT → SHORT → LONG (payoff exhale)
Paragraph length: 3–6 sentences. Never more than 7.
Each paragraph = one drawable scene.

================================================================================
THE TRANSITION BANK — BUILD YOUR OWN, NEVER REUSE A SOURCE'S
================================================================================

You need a large supply of causal connectors because this style is
connector-driven. Generate fresh variants in these FUNCTIONAL categories.
Do NOT lift a source's exact connector; write your own each time.

FUNCTION: push to next causal step
  (e.g. openers that mean "now here is what happens next")

FUNCTION: simultaneity / parallel track
  (e.g. "इसी दौरान", "उसी वक्त दूसरी तरफ", "ठीक इसी समय")

FUNCTION: consequence
  (e.g. "इसका नतीजा ये निकला", "और यहीं से", "इसी वजह से")

FUNCTION: contrast / reversal
  (e.g. "लेकिन ज़मीन पर हालात अलग थे", "कागज़ पर ये सही था, हकीकत में नहीं")

FUNCTION: forward promise
  (e.g. "ये नाम याद रखिएगा", "आगे इसका बहुत बड़ा रोल आने वाला है")

FUNCTION: recap bridge
  (e.g. "तो अब तक की तस्वीर ये बनती है")

FUNCTION: viewer question anticipation
  (e.g. "अब सवाल ये उठता है कि", "आपके मन में ये आ रहा होगा कि")

FUNCTION: importance flag
  (e.g. "ये हिस्सा सबसे ज़रूरी है")

FUNCTION: escalation
  (e.g. "और यहां से मामला पूरी तरह बदल जाता है")

HARD LIMIT: No individual connector phrase used more than TWICE per script.
HARD LIMIT: "लेकिन" starts at most 25% of paragraphs.

================================================================================
THE SPECIFICITY RULE
================================================================================

Mandatory specifics wherever facts support them:
  exact dates / exact years / exact amounts (₹, $, crore, billion) /
  exact counts (people, districts, days, km²) / named acts and sections /
  named operations / named committees and reports / named places /
  official designations / documented statements

Vague quantifiers ("कई", "बहुत सारे", "कुछ साल बाद") are allowed ONLY when the
sources genuinely do not provide a number. Never use a vague term to replace a
fact you actually have.

================================================================================
THE DIALOGUE / QUOTE SYSTEM
================================================================================

This is an explainer, so dialogue is sparse and factual — not dramatised.

Allowed, maximum 3 per script, each ≤15 words, each attributed:
  • A documented official statement
  • A documented court observation
  • A documented line from a named public document
  • A documented slogan or public declaration

Every quote must do one of:
  (a) Compress a position that would otherwise take a paragraph
  (b) Reveal intent in the speaker's own recorded words
  (c) Create documented irony (what was promised vs what followed)

❌ Never invent dialogue.
❌ Never quote a YouTube host or a transcript narrator.
❌ Never put words in a real person's mouth that are not on record.

================================================================================
ENGAGEMENT CTA SYSTEM — MID-SCRIPT COMMENT HOOKS
================================================================================

Comment CTAs are not decoration. A well-placed question converts a passive
viewer into a commenter, and comment volume drives distribution. But a FORCED
CTA breaks the causal chain and feels like begging — which is worse than none.

CORE PRINCIPLE:
  A comment CTA is only allowed at a point where the STORY ITSELF has just
  created a genuine ambiguity. You are not inserting a question — you are
  voicing the question the viewer is already forming.

  Test: If the viewer would NOT naturally be wondering this right now,
        do not ask it. Move the CTA or drop it.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FREQUENCY BY DURATION — MANDATORY TABLE
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Rule of thumb: roughly ONE comment CTA per 10 minutes, with the strongest
one always in the closing. Never more than one CTA per 8 minutes.

  Duration      | Total CTAs | Placement (approx timestamp)
  --------------|------------|------------------------------------------------
  Under 6 min   |     1      | VERDICT only — in closing
  6–9 min       |     2      | PREDICTION ~4–5 min | VERDICT in closing
  10–15 min     |     2      | PREDICTION ~6–7 min | VERDICT in closing
  16–24 min     |     3      | PREDICTION ~6–8 min | OPINION ~14–17 min |
                |            | VERDICT in closing
  25–32 min     |     3      | PREDICTION ~6–8 min | OPINION ~16–20 min |
                |            | VERDICT ~27–29 min (in closing)
  33–40 min     |     4      | PREDICTION ~7–9 min | OPINION ~17–20 min |
                |            | STAKES ~27–30 min | VERDICT in closing
  40+ min       |     5      | one per ~9 min, VERDICT last

CHARACTER-POSITION CONVERSION (since 1 min = 1,000 characters):
  A CTA planned for minute 18 lands at roughly character 18,000.
  Use this to place CTAs precisely while writing.

Example — a 28-minute script (28,000 characters) gets exactly 3 CTAs:
  CTA-1 PREDICTION  → ~char 6,000–8,000
  CTA-2 OPINION     → ~char 16,000–20,000
  CTA-3 VERDICT     → ~char 27,000–29,000

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THE 4 CTA TYPES
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▶ TYPE 1 — PREDICTION QUESTION (first CTA, ~25% mark)

  Placed IMMEDIATELY BEFORE a significant reveal. The viewer has enough
  information to form a theory but not enough to be certain. You ask them to
  commit to a guess — then the script rewards or overturns it within the next
  60–90 seconds.

  Required: the reveal MUST follow within ~1,000–1,500 characters. A prediction
  CTA whose answer arrives 10 minutes later is a broken promise.

  Shape: two named possibilities, both plausible from what's been shown.

  Pattern example:
    "आपको क्या लगता है—उसने यह फैसला मजबूरी में लिया था, या इसके पीछे कोई
     बड़ी योजना थी? कमेंट में बताइए।"

  Adapt to story type:
    History/Politics → was this a miscalculation or a deliberate strategy?
    Crime            → was this planned from the start or did it escalate?
    War/Intel        → was this an intelligence failure or an accepted risk?
    Economy/Scam     → did the regulator miss it, or look away?
    Science/Tech     → was this reckless, or the best call with what they knew?
    Biography        → conviction, or ambition?

▶ TYPE 2 — OPINION / MORAL QUESTION (middle CTA, ~60–70% mark)

  Placed when the conflict has become genuinely layered and responsibility is
  distributed across multiple actors. You ask the viewer to assign weight.

  Required: at least THREE candidates for blame/credit must already have been
  established in the script. Never ask this before the layers exist.

  Shape: three or four named parties, no obvious right answer.

  Pattern example:
    "आपकी नजर में यहां सबसे बड़ी गलती किसकी थी—सिस्टम की, अधिकारियों की,
     या उस व्यक्ति की?"

  Adapt to story type:
    History/Politics → which institution failed most: policy, enforcement, or
                       leadership?
    Crime            → the individual, the family that ignored signs, or the
                       police that filed it away?
    Disaster         → the design, the warning that was ignored, or the
                       response?
    Economy          → the operator, the auditor, or the law that allowed it?

▶ TYPE 3 — STAKES QUESTION (extra CTA, only for 33+ min scripts)

  Placed at the peak, when the scale is at maximum. You ask the viewer to
  weigh a trade-off the real actors actually faced.

  Shape: a genuine dilemma with costs on both sides.

  Pattern example:
    "अगर आप उस वक्त उस कुर्सी पर होते—आप सख्ती चुनते या बातचीत? दोनों की
     कीमत सामने थी।"

▶ TYPE 4 — FINAL VERDICT QUESTION (last CTA, in closing — ALWAYS PRESENT)

  This is your strongest comment CTA and it is MANDATORY in every script
  regardless of length. Placed after the story is complete and the present-day
  status has been stated, so the viewer answers with full information.

  Shape: three-way framing that resists a single obvious answer.

  Pattern example:
    "पूरी कहानी जानने के बाद आपका फैसला क्या है—वह हीरो था, अपराधी था,
     या हालात का शिकार?"

  Adapt to story type:
    History/Politics → necessary decision, avoidable blunder, or inevitable?
    Crime            → individual evil, or a system that made it possible?
    War/Intel        → a victory, a containment, or an unfinished job?
    Religion/Myth    → what does this teach us that still applies today?
    Science/Tech     → progress worth the cost, or a warning we ignored?
    Biography        → visionary, opportunist, or a person of their time?

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THE 12 ANTI-FORCED RULES (HARD)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

RULE 1 — NEVER MID-CAUSAL-LINK
  A CTA may only sit at a natural pause AFTER a narrative beat completes.
  Never between a cause and its consequence. Never mid-escalation.

RULE 2 — SPECIFIC, NEVER GENERIC
  The question must name the actual people, choices, or institutions from the
  story just narrated. Banned: "आपको क्या लगता है? कमेंट करें।" with no
  reference to anything specific. A CTA that could be pasted into any video
  is a failed CTA.

RULE 3 — MUST BE GENUINELY OPEN
  Do not ask a question the script has already answered, or is about to answer
  in a way that makes one option obviously correct. If there is a right
  answer, it is not a discussion question.

RULE 4 — MAXIMUM 2 SENTENCES
  One setup line plus the question, or a single question. Never a paragraph.
  Never explain why you're asking.

RULE 5 — NO REPETITION OF THEME
  Each CTA must probe a DIFFERENT dimension: prediction, then responsibility,
  then final judgment. Never ask about the same ambiguity twice.

RULE 6 — COMMENTS ONLY, MID-SCRIPT
  Mid-script CTAs ask for comments and nothing else. No "like", no
  "subscribe", no "share", no "bell icon" anywhere except the final closing
  CTA block. Like/subscribe appears exactly ONCE, at the very end.

RULE 7 — PREDICTION MUST PAY OFF FAST
  A Type 1 prediction CTA requires its reveal within ~1,000–1,500 characters.
  If the reveal is further away, move the CTA later.

RULE 8 — OPINION NEEDS ESTABLISHED LAYERS
  A Type 2 CTA requires 3+ candidate parties already introduced with their
  documented role. Otherwise the viewer has nothing to weigh.

RULE 9 — NO GUILT-TRIPPING OR BEGGING
  Banned framings: "अगर आपको वीडियो पसंद आया तो", "प्लीज कमेंट करिए",
  "हमें बताना मत भूलिए", "सिर्फ 1% लोग कमेंट करते हैं".
  You are inviting an opinion from an equal, not asking for a favour.

RULE 10 — TONE MATCHES THE MOMENT
  Never place a casual or playful CTA immediately after a death, an atrocity,
  a child victim, or a mass-casualty event. In those moments, either move the
  CTA or reframe it as a sober reflection question.

RULE 11 — NO CTA IN THE FIRST 4 MINUTES
  The viewer has not earned an opinion yet and hasn't committed to the video.
  The earliest permitted CTA is the ~4 minute mark, and only for short scripts.

RULE 12 — RESPECT THE SENSITIVITY RULES
  Never ask viewers to judge the guilt of a living person beyond what the
  documented record establishes. Never invite communal, caste, or religious
  verdicts. Reframe toward systems, decisions, and accountability instead.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
INTEGRATION WITH THE CAUSAL CHAIN
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Best placement anchors within the 20-beat arc:

  CTA-1 PREDICTION  → just before Beat 12 (THE MECHANISM REVEAL)
                      or just before Beat 9 (THE TRIGGER) in longer scripts
  CTA-2 OPINION     → just after Beat 16 (PAPER-VS-GROUND GAP)
                      or after Beat 13 (COUNTER-ESCALATION)
  CTA-3 STAKES      → just after Beat 15 (THE PEAK), 33+ min only
  CTA-4 VERDICT     → inside Beat 20, after present-day status is stated

Note: A comment CTA also functions as a retention beat. Count it in the
30-second grid as an UNANSWERED QUESTION block — because that is exactly what
it creates in the viewer's mind.

Note: The Type 4 VERDICT CTA and the "one genuine open question for the
comments" required in the ending are THE SAME element. Do not write two
separate questions in the closing.

